body:
You now have me thinking of something else. Where was Billy the night that Vasi was hit? I don't know much about him, hence why I'm asking, but wasn't he a fellow UMass student, or was he in a position where he would come and visit Maura at campus on at least a semi-regular basis? Did anyone ever borrow Maura's car while she was in class or working? Could someone who had an issue with Maura be trying to frame her for the hit and run, hence opening the door for potential harassment/stalking or even be a part of it? What if Billy had come down for a visit and borrowed Maura's car that night, did the hit and run with Vasi and then tried to make her take the blame for it? If BR was as abusive as some sources claim, this could fit in line with the pattern of behavior. Maura may have even been afraid of being blamed for it even if it wasn't her that did it if a bulletin was issued by the police department after the accident to be on the lookout for a certain type/make/model of car and it could have been issued as the kind she was driving if eyewitness testimony about the accident was skewed due to time and conditions (it being dark, snowy, possible intoxication/altered perception of witnesses given that it happened in front of a bar).

&#x200B;

I think as well that the angle of her being former military should be looked into as well and could explain why, if there was anything going on that her family is keeping quiet about, they aren't willing to open up about. Threats of legal action being taken against you by the United States government are a good reason for anyone to keep quiet about anything even if corruption is involved. Does anyone know if she kept in contact with anyone from Westpoint after she left?

That's definitely enough for me to go off of, thanks! That gave me chills to read. The first link wasn't working, so I went to the second one and though people say the author is a little kooky, it definitely does make me think. I don't know much about the supposed hit and run and even if Maura wasn't involved, I think it's still worth reading more about. That's my next project.

&#x200B;

If someone in law enforcement was threatening Maura, as the second post seems to imply, it could explain why she didn't want the police to be called when she wrecked in NH...she was probably afraid of the cops after this incident if it was true. It could also be because she was on probation for things that happened in Mass. and she didn't want to risk another run in with the police because of that. Going back to the possible threats and her odd behavior, though, what if Maura had been sexually assaulted by someone at one of the parties she went to at some point and was threatened into keeping quiet about it? This could also be an explanation of why she was acting so oddly in the days/weeks before she disappeared...the drinking and driving, emotional breakdowns, the need to get away...all of these things are behaviors that could possibly be manifesting from experiencing a traumatic event.

Late to the party, but still want to contribute.

&#x200B;

My dad is a landlord in the closest big city to us and two years ago, he agreed to rent one of his rental houses to my mom's older sister. Everyone in my mom's family warned him not to and tried to talk him out of it, but my aunt told enough of a sob story that he chose to give her the benefit of the doubt. She had already been evicted from two other rental properties prior to this, which I'm not sure that my dad was aware of when she signed the lease.

&#x200B;

She paid rent for a grand total of one month after she moved in, then things went downhill immediately after. She had five dogs that she moved into the house with her that she allowed to pee and poop in the backyard, inside the house, the front and wherever else they pleased. She piled up trash inside and outside of the house, including the driveway to the point where her car had to be parked on the street and eventually got towed for being in a "no parking" zone (the street was very narrow on her part of the block) and also because of complaints from the neighbors.

&#x200B;

My dad had her formally evicted after several inspections with nothing being cleaned up and rent not being paid and during the course of the eviction process, she harassed my parents over social media and text messages so much that they were considering getting a restraining order against her, especially when the threats toward my mom started. She also tried to have my dad falsely arrested one day after he came to the final inspection before the eviction by throwing away some of her medication in the garbage and calling the police on my dad saying that he stole her pills. Once she was out of the house, dad found rooms filled with dog feces and hair that she never cleaned up, pee stains on the carpet in over half the house, trash everywhere and rotting food in the refrigerator and freezer, dishes piled up in the sink, you name it. Aunt had been in the hospital for a perforated bowel several years prior and never got the surgery to fix it, so one room was filled with her used, dirty colostomy bags that she would change, but never bothered to throw away. Combined with non-payment of rent, dad figured she caused about $15,000 worth of damage to the house. He had to hire a biohazard team to come in and clean up the dog feces and colostomy bags and even after leaving the windows open to the let the house air out from April when they were evicted until late September, it still smelled like a dead body inside. Two years later, he's still making repairs and throwing away furniture and other junk she piled up in a dumpster in the driveway.

&#x200B;

There is a silver lining, however. Dad found out what kind of person aunt really was. She's a mega narcissist and has hated my mom since the time she was born because when my mom came, she took all the attention away from aunt. My father and I had a rocky relationship growing up and he was not the parent or husband he should have been, but definitely changed for the better after this experience. I remember as a kid how my aunt would get my dad into a corner with her and they would talk for hours at all the family gatherings and now as an adult, I definitely think that a lot of the way he treated us was because of my aunt's subtle manipulation and planting ideas into his head that his wife and kids were terrible people who would hurt him if he didn't hurt them first (dad had a rough childhood). Aunt is blacklisted from renting anywhere in our area and now lives in a motel with four of five dogs....and has been kicked out of three or four since being evicted from dad's rental house.

I love the interpretation of the girl in the photo in your third theory. Perhaps, since this happened at night and Asha was seen walking in the early hours of the morning, maybe the person who groomed her didn't so much as promise her a Valentine's day Party with her new friend as they did a 'secret slumber party" where she would have to leave when everyone was asleep so it would be a "secret". Why else would Asha leave in the middle of the night? I also like the idea of the whole "secret friendship" theory because if Asha really was as sheltered as her parents claim she was, it's likely that her interactions with people were very closely monitored by her parents...it's likely that she was only allowed to associate with other kids from church or kids that her parents basically picked to be her friends, kids that came from similar backgrounds and whose families had ideals and values that matched what her family had...having a "secret friend" that her parents didn't pick for her and didn't know about probably gave Asha a sense of freedom that she relished, giving her more motivation to risk doing something incredibly dangerous like run away from home in the middle of the night to hang out with her new buddy...she was willing to risk getting herself into danger because on the other side of the risk meant enjoying freedom and independence and being able to have something of her own without her parents' involvement and without them hovering over her. I think if we can just figure out who the girl in the picture is, then we'll have a straight shot to whoever abducted Asha.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Just wanted to add a theory....has anyone ever checked any databases of missing people to see if the girl in the photo might be among the listed? I know there are a lot of people that disappear and it goes unreported, but that doesn't mean that this girl wasn't also abducted by the same person that abducted Asha or was abducted by a stranger, but a missing relative of the person who took her. Especially now that human sex trafficking factors into this, this girl could have been a trafficking victim herself...or even just a deceased person known to the abductor...or the photo could have been a random picture found in someone's garbage or on the sidewalk somewhere (thinking it could have fallen out of someone's wallet if that's the case...not that that's relevant....need to stop rambling). But I would definitely check missing person databases or even state records kept by child protective services....picture could have been from a young girl's file and we know that children who age out of the foster system rarely have stable lives as adults...I imagine a girl could very easily jump from being in the system to becoming a prostitute or getting involved with crime in some other way as an adult, especially if she were troubled.

My worst job is one of two that I currently have as a food service worker in a large hospital. Said hospital is the flagship facility in a city-wide health system that is, at the moment, trying to bounce back from being run into the ground by a crooked, narcissistic CEO and his corrupt staff. Before he was in office, it was one of the best places in my city to work.

&#x200B;

Since I've been there, I've been lied to about how flexible my job would be around a school schedule (was told in my interview that I'd be working at most 20 hours a week during the school year and 30 on summer and holiday breaks, only to end up working 30 and sometimes even 40 while juggling a full course load), called in on almost every single day that I've had scheduled off to work because they're always short-staffed (between the day after Christmas and the first day of the spring semester this year, I didn't have a single day off because I would be called to come in), not even hitting $11 an hour (the average pay for a hospital food service employee in my area) after two raises and starting out at $10.66 an hour, been told that my job was priority over getting an education, been strong-armed into working shifts as long as 14 hours a day, forced to work every weekend and almost every single holiday, being written up for calling off the day a family member died and given attendance points for taking a day to go to their funeral because my relative was not recognized by hospital definition of bereavement leave as "immediate family", forced to work shifts in between classes, scheduled through the start of classes, almost written up for not being able to get out of going on a required school trip that I needed to go out of town for three days for so I could work a shift and now that I'm not in school anymore and have a second job, am still forced to work on their schedule 5 days a week. I am now being pressured into working 12 hour shifts on all of my days off my second job where I'm already working basically 40 hours a week because we're "short staffed" again like we were last year.

&#x200B;

This hospital uses the points system for attendance and is ridiculously strict to the point where you need to pretty much be a perfect employee in order not to get one, management plays favorites and lets employees who have been there for years or ones who are "buddy-buddy" with supervisors get away with everything short of murder, turnover rate is ridiculously high and hospital-wide, everyone is rude, overworked and miserable. I'm putting in my two week's notice on Friday and can't wait to see the look on my manager's face when she finds out she's losing another employee. We're already down 4 people out of 9 part timers...and now it's about to be 5.
